Any `En` in a character value denotes the first primitive n-th root of unity:
  En = exp(2πi/n)

The symbol `◈` indicates the principal class of the group.

Note 1: `FS` contains the classification of the irreps using the Frobenius--Schur indicator:
  `r` = real: the irrep and its complex-conjugate partner are real and identical,
  `c` = complex: the irrep and its complex-conjugate partner are complex and inequivalent,
  `q` = quaternion: the irrep and its complex-conjugate partner are complex and equivalent.

Note 2: The conjugacy classes are sorted according to the following order:
  E -> C_n (n descending) -> C2 -> i -> S_n (n decending) -> σ
  Within each order and power, elements with axes close to Cartesian axes are put first.
  Within each equi-inclination from Cartesian axes, z-inclined axes are put first, then y, then x.

Note 3: The Mulliken labels generated for the irreps in the table above are internally consistent.
  However, certain labels might differ from those tabulated elsewhere using other conventions.
  If need be, please check with other literature to ensure external consistency.

